---
import SystemPage from '../../components/v2/SystemPage.astro';
import { imagery } from '../../content/imagery';

const v2 = imagery.v2;
---

<SystemPage
	title="Modified bitumen commercial roofing"
	heroEyebrow="Commercial · multi-ply"
	heroHeading="Modified bitumen.<br />Heavy-duty asphalt."
	heroLede="Multi-ply asphaltic membrane reinforced with polymer modifiers. Built for foot-traffic, HVAC-dense, and high-stress commercial roofs. The system that doesn't get punctured."
	heroPhoto={v2.pageHeros.modifiedBitumen}
	bleedPhoto={v2.bleeds.commercial}
	bleedHeading="Two plies thick.<br />Built to take a beating."
	overview={{
		eyebrow: 'What mod-bit is',
		heading: 'Asphalt + polymer, installed in plies.',
		body: "Modified bitumen is a multi-ply asphaltic system — a base sheet plus one or two cap sheets, each reinforced with polyester or fiberglass and modified with SBS (rubber-like) or APP (plastic-like) polymers. Heat-welded with a torch, hot-mopped with asphalt, or self-adhered. The result is a thick, redundant membrane that takes foot traffic and rooftop equipment service without puncturing."
	}}
	keyPoints={[
		{
			heading: 'Where mod-bit is the right call.',
			body: "Mod-bit fits buildings with heavy rooftop equipment, frequent service traffic, or owners who prioritize puncture resistance over reflectivity. Hospitals, data centers, manufacturing — anywhere people are walking the roof regularly.",
			bullets: ['Foot-traffic-heavy roofs', 'HVAC-dense, equipment-heavy buildings', 'Hospitals, manufacturing, data centers', 'Where puncture-resistance > reflectivity'],
			photo: v2.systemTiles.modBit,
			photoSide: 'left',
		},
		{
			heading: 'SBS vs APP — and how we install.',
			body: "SBS modifiers are flexible — easier to install in cold weather, friendlier to torch crews, more forgiving on rolling decks. APP modifiers are stiffer and more heat-resistant — preferred for hot-mop or torch-down on flat decks with no movement. Heritage runs cold-applied, hot-applied, or torch installs depending on the specification and the building.",
			bullets: ['SBS modifier (flexible, cold-friendly)', 'APP modifier (stiff, heat-resistant)', 'Cold-applied, hot-applied, or torched'],
			photo: v2.bleeds.workmanship,
			photoSide: 'right',
		},
	]}
	stats={[
		{ value: '20–25', label: 'Years typical service life' },
		{ value: '2-ply', label: 'Standard system thickness' },
		{ value: 'Class A', label: 'Fire rating · UL 790' },
		{ value: 'High', label: 'Puncture resistance' },
	]}
	projectPhotos={[v2.projects[5], v2.projects[6], v2.projects[3], v2.projects[7]]}
	projectCaptions={['Mod-bit, healthcare', 'Mod-bit + parapet detail', '2-ply replacement, manufacturing', 'Mod-bit with rooftop equipment']}
	faq={[
		{ q: 'Mod-bit vs TPO — which is the right call?', a: "<p>Foot traffic. If people are on the roof regularly — HVAC service, satellite work, plumbing access — mod-bit takes the abuse better. TPO can puncture under hard-edged tools dropped on it; a 2-ply mod-bit shrugs them off. If foot traffic is rare, TPO usually wins on reflectivity and install cost.</p>" },
		{ q: 'Are torch installs still common?', a: '<p>Less than they used to be. Cold-applied and self-adhered systems have eaten a big chunk of the torch market because they remove the open-flame hazard. Heritage runs torch installs on jobs where the spec calls for it and the building tolerates it; otherwise we default to cold-applied or hot-mopped.</p>' },
		{ q: 'How does it compare to BUR (built-up)?', a: "<p>BUR is the predecessor — multiple plies of asphalt-saturated felt with hot asphalt between them, topped with gravel. Mod-bit replaced most BUR work because the polymer-modified cap sheets carry better tensile strength and the install is less labor-intensive. BUR is still alive on some specs, but mod-bit is what we install on new construction.</p>" },
		{ q: 'How long does mod-bit last?', a: '<p>20–25 years on a properly-installed 2-ply system, longer on systems with a granulated cap or with periodic flood-coating maintenance. Like any roof, the failure point is usually flashings and penetrations, not the field membrane.</p>' },
		{ q: 'Can you install over my existing roof?', a: '<p>Sometimes. Mod-bit recovers well over an existing built-up or smooth-surface roof if the deck is sound. We tell you yes or no on the inspection.</p>' },
	]}
	ctaHeading="Mod-bit walkthrough,<br />scope, and bid in a week."
	ctaLede="Free roof assessment. Real answer on whether mod-bit fits your building."
	ctaPhoto={v2.pageHeros.commercial}
/>
